# AGENTS.md

{{synopsis}}. The owner reviews every change.
Write code that {{reader}} can read. Setup is in `README.md`.

## Checks

`scripts/check` runs every gate: Ormolu, HLint, a search for switched-off checks, a build with
warnings as errors, and the tests. The git hook and CI run it too.

- Work in small steps and keep `scripts/check` green after each one.
- Done means `scripts/check` is green. Show the command and the end of its output.
- When a check stays red after two honest attempts, stop and report what you tried.
- Fix the cause of a red check. Gate files change only with the owner's explicit approval:
  `scripts/check`, `.githooks/`, `.github/`, `.hlint.yaml`, `.claude/`, the warning and extension
  settings in `{{package}}.cabal` and `cabal.project`, and existing tests.

```sh
cabal test --test-options='--match "banner"'               # one test by name
ormolu --mode inplace $(find app src test -name '*.hs')    # format
```

Sandboxed agents: `cabal` writes to `~/.cache/cabal` and `~/.local/state/cabal`, and `cabal update`
and `gh` need the network. Without these, `scripts/check` fails for reasons outside the code.

## Haskell

Read `CODING_STANDARDS.md` before writing or reviewing Haskell.

The checks enforce imports with an explicit list or a qualifier, extensions set only in
`{{package}}.cabal` (no `LANGUAGE` pragmas), and the `.hlint.yaml` bans on partial functions. HLint
bans by name, so names like `read`, `last` and `error` are taken: call your own `readConfig` or
`lastValue`.

## Layout

- Logic goes in the library under `src/`. `app/Main.hs` only calls it.
- `test/` is an hspec suite. Every `*Spec.hs` module that exports `spec` runs automatically.
- List every new module in `{{package}}.cabal` (`exposed-modules` or `other-modules`).
- `cabal.project` pins `index-state`. Bump it on purpose when you need newer Hackage packages.
- `with-compiler` in `cabal.project` names the GHC version. `scripts/check` and CI read it there.
  To change GHC, update it together with `tested-with` and the `base` bound in
  `{{package}}.cabal`.

## Writing

For chat replies, commits, PRs, code comments and docs. Many readers are not native English
speakers.

- Short sentences and plain words. Lead with the result. Then what the reader needs to act: what
  changed, what failed, what is left, what you did not verify.
- Say what a technical idea does before you name it.
- A chat reply fits one screen. Use a table for parallel facts and a numbered list for steps.
  Keep every risk and open question.
- Name files by repo-relative path.
- Docs state rules and pointers, never project status. Status goes stale.
- Commits and PRs describe the change only, with no tool or AI attribution.
- PR title: `type(scope): what changed`, at most 72 characters. CI checks it. PR body: fill
  `.github/pull_request_template.md`, one line per slot. One concern per PR, about 200 changed
  lines at most. Squash merges make the PR title and body the commit on `main`.
